# 5. Folder APIs

The Folder APIs manage the logical organization of user documents.

KnowledgeOS follows a **single-level folder hierarchy**, meaning folders cannot contain subfolders. This simplifies navigation while providing sufficient organization for most users.

Every user automatically receives the following system folders:

- Uploaded
- Quick Notes
- Favorites
- Recently Deleted

Users may additionally create custom folders.

---

## Folder API Flow

```text
React Frontend

↓

Folder API

↓

Authentication

↓

Authorization

↓

PostgreSQL

↓

JSON Response
```

---

## Get All Folders

```http
GET /api/v1/folders
```

Purpose:

Returns all folders belonging to the authenticated user.

Includes:

- System folders
- Custom folders
- Document counts
- Folder metadata

Example Response

```json
[
  {
    "id": "...",
    "name": "Uploaded",
    "document_count": 18,
    "is_system_folder": true
  },
  {
    "id": "...",
    "name": "Research Papers",
    "document_count": 9,
    "is_system_folder": false
  }
]
```

---

## Create Folder

```http
POST /api/v1/folders
```

Purpose:

Creates a new custom folder.

Request

```json
{
    "name": "System Design"
}
```

Validation

- Folder name cannot be empty.
- Maximum folder name length enforced.
- Duplicate folder names are not allowed for the same user.
- System folder names are reserved.

Response

```http
201 Created
```

---

## Rename Folder

```http
PATCH /api/v1/folders/{folder_id}
```

Purpose

Renames an existing custom folder.

Only custom folders can be renamed.

System folders cannot be renamed.

---

## Delete Folder

```http
DELETE /api/v1/folders/{folder_id}
```

Purpose

Deletes a custom folder.

Business Rule

KnowledgeOS never deletes documents when a folder is removed.

Before deleting the folder, all documents are automatically moved to the user's **Uploaded** system folder.

Workflow

```text
Delete Folder Request

↓

Move Documents

↓

Uploaded Folder

↓

Delete Folder

↓

Return Success
```

---

## Get Folder Details

```http
GET /api/v1/folders/{folder_id}
```

Returns

- Folder metadata
- Total documents
- Creation date
- Last updated
- Folder type

---

## Get Folder Documents

```http
GET /api/v1/folders/{folder_id}/documents
```

Returns all documents inside a folder.

Supports:

- Pagination
- Sorting
- Filtering
- Search

